# Optional file authorship

This rule is an opt-in reusable profile. It does not apply to public
contributions or derived projects unless the repository explicitly adopts it.

Git history and the repository license are the default sources for authorship,
provenance, and reuse rights. Do not add per-file names or email addresses by
default.

## Requirement after explicit adoption

- Every new human-maintained repository file MUST include author information
  only after this convention has been explicitly adopted.
- Apply this requirement when creating a new file, including a new file
  produced by splitting or extracting content from an existing file.
- Do not treat a simple rename or move of an existing file as new authorship.
- Do not retroactively add authorship metadata to unrelated existing files as
  part of an unrelated change.

## Author identity

- When editing an existing file, preserve its author information unless the
  current change explicitly requires that metadata to be corrected or updated.
- For a newly created file, use the effective Git identity for the repository
  when both values are configured:
  - `git config --get user.name`
  - `git config --get user.email`
- If either value is unavailable, surface the limitation rather than silently
  using an unrelated identity.
- If the repository convention also includes a handle and the applicable
  handle is established by existing repository metadata, include it consistently.
- Never invent a name, email address, username, or handle.
- Do not identify an AI agent as the author or co-author unless the repository
  explicitly requires that attribution.

## Representation

Use the file format's normal, valid mechanism for authorship.

Prefer this order:

1. a standard or schema-defined author field when the format explicitly
   supports one;
2. otherwise, a valid comment using the file format's established comment
   syntax;
3. if neither is valid, use an established adjacent metadata mechanism when
   the repository or format provides one.

Examples:

```text
Python / Shell / YAML / TOML / Dockerfile
# Author: Name <email> (@handle)

JavaScript / TypeScript / Go / Java / C-like source
// Author: Name <email> (@handle)

SQL
-- Author: Name <email> (@handle)

HTML / XML / Markdown
<!-- Author: Name <email> (@handle) -->
```

When a format has a standard non-comment author field, use that field instead
of adding a redundant comment.

## Placement

- Place authorship metadata near the beginning of the file where it is easy
  for a human reader to discover.
- Preserve format-required leading content.
- A shebang MUST remain the first line when required.
- An encoding declaration, XML declaration, document frontmatter, or other
  syntax with a required position MUST remain valid.
- When required leading metadata exists, place the author information at the
  earliest valid location after or within that metadata according to the
  format's conventions.
- Follow an established repository placement pattern when one exists.

## Validity and schema safety

- Authorship metadata MUST NOT make a file syntactically invalid or violate
  the file's schema.
- Do not add comments to formats that do not allow comments.
- Do not invent an `author` key in JSON, manifests, configuration files, or
  structured payloads unless the governing schema explicitly permits it.
- Do not add unsupported metadata merely to satisfy this rule mechanically.
- When in-file authorship is impossible without violating a required format or
  schema, preserve file validity and use an established standard metadata
  mechanism if one exists.
- If no valid authorship mechanism exists, surface the limitation rather than
  corrupting the file.

## Generated and externally managed files

Do not manually inject authorship metadata into files whose contents are
controlled by another tool or external source when doing so would be unstable,
overwritten, invalid, or misleading.

This includes, when applicable:

- lock files;
- generated artifacts;
- vendored or third-party files;
- compiled or binary files;
- caches;
- files copied verbatim from an external authoritative source;
- machine-generated files whose generator owns the complete file content.

If a generator provides a supported template or metadata mechanism for
authorship, configure that mechanism instead of post-editing generated output.

## Existing conventions

- Preserve a more specific repository or format convention when one exists.
- Do not create multiple competing author headers in the same file.
- When a new file is based substantially on an existing repository file,
  preserve legally or historically relevant authorship and attribution rather
  than replacing it blindly.
- Authorship metadata does not replace Git history, copyright notices, license
  headers, or legally required attribution.
